[Music]  in a 1677 letter to the royal society of  london  the dutch tradesman and microbiology  pioneer anthony van lievenhoek  described the strange appendages he had  found on a creature in a sample of  rainwater  he observed the organism was quote  provided with divers incredibly thin  little feet or little legs which were  moved  very nimbly we don't know exactly what  organism he was describing  just that it was some sort of ciliated  protozoan  but this letter is important because the  little legs  he was describing constitute one of the  earliest descriptions  of a cellular organelle  levenhook was referring to cilia the  name would be bestowed in 1786  by otto muller who took inspiration from  the word psyllium which translates  to eyelash in latin today we know that  cilia  are a bundle of microtubules and that  the synchronized beating of  motile cilia can create currents in the  water  we can often see cilia in action waving  around  along the edges of a single cell  eukaryote as they move around  or even crowning more complex organisms  like rotifers to help them gather food  so sometimes cilia are a way to move  around  and sometimes they're a way to eat and  sometimes they are both  but sometimes they let an organism lead  the most  ideal lifestyle of all one  where you just hang out and let the food  come to you  one group of ciliates that manages to  strive toward  that ideal are the peritrix ciliates  a diverse group of protists that contain  around a thousand  species that number making about a  seventh of all  ciliates we currently know about  peritric ciliates  are characterized by their vase-like  shape  or depending on where you're from  vaz-like shape  which opens into an enlarged mouth-like  structure  called the peristome that is lined with  what else  cilia one of the most well-studied  peritrix ciliates  are vorticella whose cilia you can see  waving around here  their peristomes are lined with two  bands of cilia one to generate water  currents and to draw food towards their  mouthy thing and another to filter  through the particles  and it turns out that all that movement  is quite effective  scientists measuring some vorticella  found that even when their cilia  are only 17 micrometers long  they can bring in particles that were  450  micrometers away imagine  if they were a delicious snack 50 feet  away from you  and all you had to do was a wave your  arms in the air  morticella truly living the dream  we featured vorticella on a recent  episode where we followed them  in real time as they dealt with rude  neighbors  and other little microcosmic things  we were able to film that episode  because vorticella belong  to the larger order of peritrix ciliates  called  the sicilia which as the name suggests  are all scessile that means for most of  their life  sicilied peritrix ciliates are found  attached to other surfaces  be they rock plant or something else  in contrast the rest of the paratroop  ciliates are grouped together as members  of the free swimming mobilita  of course while these distinctions are  useful things are not necessarily as  neat as  these organisms swim and these organisms  don't  sicilid paritrix live their lives in one  of  two distinct forms the dominant one  the one that defines them is their adult  sessile form  which is called the trophant stage the  trophant cecilid  is attached to a surface like the  forticella using its long  stock to stake its place and its body  lacks any cilia except for those  distinct bands  around their peristome but in their  youth  cecilia peritrix are free swimmers  called teletrokes  in contrast to their trophon stages  these teletrokes  usually don't feed so their oral  cilia are tucked away but they do have  a single band of cilia around their  bodies  called the trocal band which helps them  swim around  until they find something to attach to  while the teletroke is generally  associated with  young cecilia peritrix a trophant  can return to this stage if it needs to  find a new home  like this campanella that's decided to  leave its stock  and swim off somewhere new  [Music]  and just like cecilia peritrix do  sometimes move around  there are so-called mobilitrics with an  adhesive  disk that allows them to attach to  things  but unlike cecilids mobilids never  lose their trocal cilia that help them  swim around  and when they swim around they really do  put the mobile and mobilid  i can barely keep this one in the frame  the evolutionary lines connecting these  two orders are vague there have been  hypotheses that mobilide paritrix  evolved from the swimming teletroke  stage of the cecilia life cycle  but attempts to use molecular phylogeny  to verify that prediction at a genetic  level  have come up with varied results so  there's a chance  that these different organisms are  related but they  also may be two unrelated groups of  organisms who  stumbled on the same ciliated oral  structure  through convergent evolution  order they belong to the peritrix  ciliates  that do attach to a substrate make for  an interesting study  part of it is the way they bring their  own drama  to what seems like a dull sedentary life  you might have noticed this in our real  time episode where the vorticella  rapidly contracted and responsed to  passing organisms  when this happens a vorticella's stock  can collapse to around 20 to 40 percent  of its original length  in milliseconds a fast movement that is  followed eventually  by a much slower and more chill  relaxation  back to full length in his own  observation of vorticella  leavenhook noted that he found this site  mightily diverting  [Music]  but the choices that peritrix ciliates  make  in where to set up their stocks can be  mightily diverting  as well sometimes it's the typical  substrates things like  plants or rocks but sometimes it's  animals  sometimes these interactions can become  more fraught  mobilid paretrix for example are known  to infect  fishes and amphibians creating both  ecological  and economic challenges but when these  interactions aren't harmful  you get something called epibiosis  where an organism lives on another  organism without being parasitic  and judging by the many varied animal  homes that peritrix ciliates have chosen  they're not particularly picky like  these ciliates  that have found a place on this water  flea to cozy up to  some have been found in the gills of  blue crabs  others on tardigrades and snails  and pelagic diatoms and the antenna of  european crayfish  the list truly goes on  [Music]  in a way that has delighted us for  centuries  using those tiny little legs in their  mouths and along their bodies  peritric ciliates have managed to carve  out a very specific  and some might say ideal way of life  that seems to have helped them survive  just about anywhere thank you for coming  on this journey with us  as we explore the unseen world that  surrounds us  journey to the micro cosmos is produced  by complexly  and this is a bit of a left turn but if  you are a us citizen and want to not  only  keep imagining the world complexly but  also  have a voice in your government check  out how to vote in every state  the rules for voting are different  depending on where you live in the u.s  so we've made a whole series explaining  how to vote in each state  as well as some advice for special cases  like territories and overseas voters  check out our 2020 guide at youtube.com  how to vote in every state if you like  what we do here at journey to the  microcosmos  we deeply appreciate that and we want to  thank all the people  on the screen right now for helping make  that possible these  are our patrons on patreon if you want  to join them  check out patreon.com journey2micro  if you want to see more from our master  of microscopes james weiss check out jam  and germs on instagram  and if you want to see more from us  there's usually a subscribe button  somewhere nearby  you

